Q. What is AWS CodeCommit (Git Repository)? Features?
A:	AWS CodeCommit is a fully managed Git-based source control service by AWS that lets you securely store, manage, and version control code in private repositories.
Feature
Description
Secure
Data is encrypted in transit (HTTPS) and at rest (AWS KMS)
Version Control
Uses Git — supports branches, commits, tags
Highly Available
Managed by AWS, built for durability and availability
Team Collaboration
Supports pull requests, code reviews, and triggers
Integration
Works seamlessly with CodeBuild, CodeDeploy, CodePipeline


Q. Difference between CodeCommit & Github?
A: 	
CodeCommit → AWS-managed private Git repository service.
GitHub → Publicly popular Git hosting platform with open-source + private repos, community, and integrations.

Q. What is AWS CodeBuild (CI Build Service)
A: 	AWS CodeBuild is a fully managed CI service that compiles code, runs tests, and produces build artifacts without you managing servers.
Feature
Description
Build Automation
Automatically compiles code and runs unit tests
buildspec.yml
Defines build steps and environment
Artifacts Support
Generates build outputs (e.g., .jar, .zip, .war)
Scalable & On-Demand
Builds run in parallel and scale automatically
Secure
Uses AWS IAM, logs to CloudWatch, encrypted storage
Integration
Works with CodeCommit, GitHub, S3, CodePipeline, Jenkins


Q. What is Artifact?
A:	An artifact is the output generated after a build process — such as JAR, WAR, Docker image, or ZIP file — which is later used for deployment.

